#################
# Gitattributes #
#################
# [OVERVIEW] “.gitattributes” — a file with settings that Git applied
# when users run Git commands inside repository:
# https://git-scm.com/docs/gitattributes
#
# [USAGE][NOTE] Not all features from the file “.gitconfig” are available in the file “.gitattributes”.
#
# [USAGE][NOTE] The file “.gitattributes” haven’t the setting for printing Non-ASCII filenames to console.
# Adding the setting “quotePath false” to the global configuration via the command “git config --global core.quotePath false”
# or to the local configuration via the command “git config core.quotePath false” is required.


# [LEARN][LINE_ENDINGS] “CRLF vs. LF: Normalizing Line Endings in Git” — the detailed manual about line endings in Git:
# https://www.aleksandrhovhannisyan.com/blog/crlf-vs-lf-normalizing-line-endings-in-git/


# [GIT][LINE_ENDINGS][INFO] Run the command “git config --system --list” to see the default value of the option “core.autocrlf”.
# On UNIX the default value should be “false”, on Windows — “true”:
# https://stackoverflow.com/a/46694814/5951529
# https://stackoverflow.com/a/48230871/5951529
#
# [GIT][LINE_ENDINGS][APPVEYOR][INFO] Git on AppVeyor automatically set “git config --global core.autocrlf input”:
# https://www.appveyor.com/docs/windows-images-software/#version-control-systems


# [GIT][LINE_ENDINGS][INFO] The command “git ls-files --eol” shows line endings of all non-ignored project files.
# Output of the command looks like this: “i/lf    w/crlf  attr/text=auto  KiraFile.txt”.
# A file may have different line endings in the Git Index and in the working tree (i.e. in the filesystem):
# https://www.aleksandrhovhannisyan.com/blog/crlf-vs-lf-normalizing-line-endings-in-git/#verifying-line-endings-in-git-for-any-file
#
# 1. “i/lf” — line endings of the “KiraFile.txt” in the Git Index.
#
# 2. “w/crlf” — line endings of the “KiraFile.txt” in the filesystem.


# [GUIDE] Resolving all problems with line endings in your projects.
# I want always use LF line endings except files where Windows CRLF line endings required.
#
# ---
#
# Set line endings on the local filesystem:
#
# 1. Add the file “.editorconfig” to the root of the project. The file should contain these lines:
#
# ```ini
# [*]
# end_of_line = lf
# ```
#
# If the project contains Windows-specific files required CRLF line endings like “.bat” files,
# add to your “.editorconfig” lines like this:
#
# ```ini
# [*.bat]
# end_of_line = crlf
# ```
#
# 2. Install EditorConfig plugin for your IDE. Sublime Text users should use editorconfig-sublime:
# https://github.com/sindresorhus/editorconfig-sublime
# EditorConfig plugins for IDE automatically set line endings from “.editorconfig” by saving files.
#
# 3. To check that all files in your project conform your “.editorconfig” settings
# use ECLint and/or EditorConfig-Checker.
# Run “eclint check” and/or “editorconfig-checker”:
# https://github.com/jednano/eclint#check
# https://editorconfig-checker.github.io/
#
# [FIXME][TRY] editorconfig-checker
#
# 4. Problems in sources found by “eclint check” and/or “editorconfig-checker” fix manually.
# Run ECLint command “eclint fix” for all compiled/generated files except binaries
# to make them match settings from the file “.editorconfig”.
# Among other things, ECLint converts “w/crlf” to “w/lf”.
# Don’t run ECLint for binaries, they don’t need changing line endings.
# Don’t run ECLint for sources files without a backup, it can be unsafe.
# https://github.com/jednano/eclint#fix
# After “eclint fix” run “eclint check” and/or “editorconfig-checker” again to make sure that all problems fixed.
#
# [FIXME][TRY] Prettier instead of EditorConfig:
# https://prettier.io/docs/configuration#editorconfig
#
# ---
#
# Set line endings on Git Index:
#
# 1. Add the file “.gitattributes” to the root folder of your repository. The content of the file “.gitattributes”:
#
# ```text
# * text=auto eol=lf
# ```
#
# If the project contains files with Windows-specific line endings like “*.bat”,
# preserving CRLF line endings like “*.bat eol=crlf” in the file “.gitattributes” is required to prevent bugs:
#
# ```text
# *.bat eol=crlf
# ```
#
# https://rehansaeed.com/gitattributes-best-practices/#line-endings
#
# [OPTION][LINE_ENDINGS] “* text=auto eol=lf” — automatically convert line endings
# in all non-ignored non-binary files in Git Index to UNIX format “LF” for cross-platform line endings compatibility.
# https://ru.stackoverflow.com/a/691545/199934
# https://rehansaeed.com/gitattributes-best-practices/#line-endings
# https://git-scm.com/docs/gitattributes#_text
#
# [GIT][LINE_ENDINGS][INFO] “* text=auto” is the equivalent of the global setting “git config --global core.autocrlf input”:
# https://stackoverflow.com/a/73184844/5951529
# https://www.aleksandrhovhannisyan.com/blog/crlf-vs-lf-normalizing-line-endings-in-git/#summary-git-config-vs-gitattributes
#
# [GIT][LINE_ENDINGS][INFO] If the file “.gitattributes” contains solely the line “* text=auto”,
# whenever users run “git checkout”, Git on Windows converts LF to CRLF,
# and after each “git add” Windows users get the warning “warning: CRLF will be replaced by LF in <file-name>”:
# https://stackoverflow.com/a/1967986/5951529
# The setting “eol=lf” is required to prevent conversion LF to CRLF.
#
# [GIT][LINE_ENDINGS][INFO] If “text” is set but neither of those variables is,
# the default is “eol=crlf” on Windows and “eol=lf” on all other platforms:
# https://git-scm.com/docs/gitattributes#Documentation/gitattributes.txt-Unspecified-1-1
#
# [GIT][LINE_ENDINGS][INFO] “eol=lf” requires the setting “text”:
# https://stackoverflow.com/a/46592252/5951529
#
# 2. If the file “.gitattributes” exists, global and local settings “core.autocrlf false”
# in files “.gitconfig” and “.git/config” isn’t needed.
# Settings from “.gitattributes” has better priority than settings
# from global “.gitconfig” and local “.git/config”:
# https://git-scm.com/docs/gitattributes#Documentation/gitattributes.txt-Unspecified-1
#
# 3. If you add “.gitattributes” to old repository, files that you added before “.gitattributes” still have
# “i/crlf” line endings in Git index if they had “i/crlf” in the past.
# Run the command “git add --renormalize .” to convert all line endings to “i/lf” in the Git Index:
# https://www.aleksandrhovhannisyan.com/blog/crlf-vs-lf-normalizing-line-endings-in-git/#a-simple-gitattributes-config
# https://stackoverflow.com/a/47580886/5951529
* text=auto eol=lf
